# November 22, 2026: Utah, a second statewide scatter. The state had 35 places, most round Tintic, the West Desert and
# the places the first scatter (ut_pbty) chose; this batch adds 18 more from Corinne and Brigham City to Pine Valley,
# Panguitch, Torrey, Helper, Vernal, Cub Creek and the Colorado River.
# Facts come from the linked sources, opened in this session: Wikipedia (read as raw text), the pages of whoever runs each
# place (the Bureau of Land Management, the National Park Service, the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints'
# historic-site pages, Tooele County's Benson Grist Mill page, Helper City's museum page), the Utah State Historic
# Preservation Office's 2022 Spring City Register form, Utah Humanities, Moab Happenings, KSL, the Deseret News and HMdb.org.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'ut_bade','label':'Utah: a second scatter, from Corinne to Pine Valley and Cub Creek','added':'2026-11-22 21:33'}
LDS_SG=L('Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints: St. George Tabernacle (hours)','https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/learn/locations/st-george-tabernacle')
LDS_HAM=L('Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints: Hamblin Home (hours)','https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/learn/locations/hamblin-home')
LDS_PV=L('Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints: Pine Valley Chapel','https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/learn/locations/pine-valley-chapel')
PLACES=[
# ---- south-west ----
N('fort-pearce-ut','Fort Pearce','Washington','ut','fort','ruins',37.00783,-113.41184,'exact','public','walk','forts','1866–1873',
  'A stone guard post built by Latter-day Saint militia on a bluff above Fort Pearce Wash, over the only spring for miles, during the raiding of 1865 to 1872 that Utah calls the Black Hawk War. The BLM says a work party of 20 began it on December 4, 1866 and spent nearly a month quarrying sandstone for the post and a stock corral; Wikipedia says six men began it and nineteen built the corral in 1869. No fight is known to have happened here; the post was manned now and then until 1873.',
  'The roofless dry-laid or mud-mortared sandstone post, about 42 by 22 feet with walls 7 to 8 feet high, one doorway, a partition into two rooms, small flanking walls off two corners and ten gun slits near the tops of the walls (BLM description). The BLM calls it one of only three stone guard posts of that war still standing. Interpretive signs.',
  'A guard post above the only spring for miles, built for raids that never came.',
  'Bureau of Land Management public land (St. George Field Office), in the Warner Ridge / Fort Pearce area of critical environmental concern. The BLM says the site is generally open year-round by the unpaved Warner Valley Road, about 12 miles south of St. George, with signed turns and a parking area half a mile past the last turn. No water. The Warner Valley dinosaur track site is nearby.',
  [W('Fort Pearce (Washington, Utah)'),L('BLM: Fort Pearce Historic Site','https://www.blm.gov/visit/fort-pearce-historic-site')],
  conf='mixed',th=[37.00741,-113.41102],thn='Fort Pearce trailhead parking',brick='no'),
N('pine-valley-chapel','Pine Valley Chapel and Tithing Office','Pine Valley','ut','worship','standing',37.39189,-113.51461,'exact','open','drive','','1868 or 1872–73; tithing office 1880s',
  'A white two-storey wooden meetinghouse in the pines of Pine Valley, where sawmills supplied timber for the St. George Temple and Tabernacle and the Salt Lake Tabernacle\'s organ pipes. The dates differ: Wikipedia gives 1868 and calls it the church\'s oldest meetinghouse in continuous use; the church\'s own page says settlers began it in 1872 and finished in a year. The shipbuilder Ebenezer Bryce is usually said to have built the roof like an upturned ship; the church\'s page says he may have helped but the roof used the ordinary methods of the day.',
  'The two-storey frame chapel, the upper floor the worship room and the lower once a schoolroom and community hall, and east of it the red brick tithing office of the 1880s, where tithes paid in goods were stored. The church says the roof and windows have been replaced but the original structure remains, and an attic room holds old photographs and artifacts. Summer tours take in both buildings.',
  'A New England-style meetinghouse said to be built like a ship turned over.',
  'Owned by the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints, 50 West Main Street, Pine Valley; still used for Sunday services. The church\'s page says tours are given daily in summer and to contact the chapel, (435) 574-3202, for current hours. Only the first floor takes wheelchairs. Pine Valley is up a mountain road from Central; winter conditions not checked.',
  [W('Pine Valley Chapel and Tithing Office'),LDS_PV],
  conf='mixed',brick='some',hours='Tours daily in summer; ring the chapel for times'),
N('jacob-hamblin-home','Jacob Hamblin Home','Santa Clara','ut','landmark','standing',37.13361,-113.66025,'exact','open','drive','','1863',
  'After a flood swept away Santa Clara in 1862, missionaries built this two-storey sandstone house in 1863 for Jacob Hamblin, head of the church\'s mission to the Paiutes, and his family; it served as the mission\'s headquarters. Built into a hillside, it can be entered on both floors. The family gave it to Utah State Parks in 1959, which restored it, and the church took it over in 1974.',
  'The sandstone house with a porch across the front on each floor, and matching rooms at each end, each with its own fireplace and stair, for Hamblin\'s two wives (Wikipedia). The church says the house and furnishings are restored to the 1860s, and missionaries give free tours.',
  '',
  'Owned and run by the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints, 3325 Hamblin Drive, Santa Clara. Free guided tours; telephone (435) 677-6230. Hours from the church\'s page.',
  [W('Jacob Hamblin House'),LDS_HAM],brick='no',hours='Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 6 p.m.; Sunday noon to 6 p.m.'),
N('st-george-tabernacle','St. George Tabernacle','St. George','ut','worship','standing',37.10806,-113.58278,'exact','open','drive','','1863–1876',
  'Begun in June 1863 under Brigham Young\'s direction, with labour donated or paid from tithing by settlers across southern Utah, and opened in May 1876. Designed by Miles Romney, it served as both church and courthouse and has held concerts, conferences and services of many faiths. The church restored it inside and out to its 19th-century look in 2016 to 2018.',
  'Red sandstone walls 2.5 feet thick, a clock and bell tower about 140 feet high, and inside a hall with ceilings about 29 feet up, a plaster cornice, twenty columns, a gallery on three sides reached by two spiral stairs, and window glass brought from California (Wikipedia). Guided tours and exhibits of tools and ornament used in building it.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints, 18 South Main Street, on the town square. Guided tours; telephone (435) 677-6230. Hours from the church\'s page.',
  [W('St. George Tabernacle'),LDS_SG],brick='no',hours='Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 6 p.m.; Sunday noon to 6 p.m.'),
N('parowan-old-rock-church','Parowan Old Rock Church (Parowan Meetinghouse)','Parowan','ut','worship','standing',37.84123,-112.82881,'exact','open','drive','','1861–1870',
  'A stone meetinghouse built in 1861 to 1870 by local men, with meetings held in the basement from 1867 and the first in the 800-seat chapel when Brigham Young came in April 1870. The church left it after 1914, when a new meetinghouse went up on the same lot; the city took it over about 1920 and handed it to the Daughters of Utah Pioneers.',
  'The rock meetinghouse with two separate stairs and entrances, once one for men and one for women. It is now the Parowan Old Rock Church Museum, which Wikipedia says gives daily tours from Memorial Day to Labor Day. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Cared for by the Daughters of Utah Pioneers on city land, in the centre of Parowan. No page of the museum\'s own could be opened (the city\'s page for it answered 404), so the summer tour season is from Wikipedia and hours were not checked.',
  [W('Parowan Meetinghouse')],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('beaver-county-courthouse-ut','Old Beaver County Courthouse','Beaver','ut','civic','standing',38.2772,-112.64025,'site','open','drive','','1876–1882',
  'Built from 1876 to 1882 for $15,000 by William Stokes, once U.S. marshal at Beaver, it held the Second Judicial Court for all of southern Utah as well as county offices and records. The Register form gave no architect; Wikipedia credits Richard Kletting. A vault and jail were added at the back later.',
  'A red brick Victorian courthouse on a stone foundation (Wikipedia describes it once as two storeys on whitewashed sandstone and once as three on black igneous rock). It is now the Beaver courthouse museum of the Daughters of Utah Pioneers, which Wikipedia says opens in summer. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Run as a museum by the Daughters of Utah Pioneers, in the centre of Beaver. Their directory page could not be opened, so hours were not checked; the outside can be seen from the street any time.',
  [W('Beaver County Courthouse (Utah)')],conf='mixed',brick='yes',hours='?'),
N('panguitch-historic-district','Panguitch Historic District','Panguitch','ut','town','standing',37.82264,-112.43558,'area','public','walk','','1890–1930',
  'The old centre of Panguitch, a Latter-day Saint town laid out on a grid, listed as a district with 379 contributing buildings, most of them houses of 1890 to 1930 and a commercial block in the middle. Wikipedia says most are of red brick, many of a deep red, soft-edged brick made near Panguitch from 1915 to 1940, and many houses follow a local pattern: a square plan, a pyramid roof and projecting bays.',
  'Street after street of red brick houses. The grandest is the Ira Hatch House of 1896 at 329 East Center, two storeys with a square tower, designed by Richard Kletting. The Carnegie library and the Social Hall are listed separately. Seen from the streets; most buildings are private homes.',
  'A whole town of the same deep red local brick.',
  'Public streets in a working town; the houses are private, so look from the street. The pin marks the middle of the district.',
  [W('Panguitch Historic District')],brick='yes'),
# ---- centre ----
N('torrey-log-church-schoolhouse','Torrey Log Church and Schoolhouse','Torrey','ut','worship','standing',38.29929,-111.42008,'exact','view','drive','','1898',
  'Torrey\'s first church and first school, finished in 1898. It was the school until 1917 and the meetinghouse until 1928, and the local Daughters of Utah Pioneers met in it into the 1970s. Given to them on condition it left church land, it was moved about 100 yards west in 1991 and restored.',
  'A one-room building of sawn logs with half-dovetailed corners, about 37 by 21 feet, with pedimented window heads, a flared hip roof and a bell tower over the door; inside it is open to the rafters and lined with beaded boards (Wikipedia). The state marker calls it the only log meetinghouse known still standing in Utah. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Daughters of Utah Pioneers, who use it for meetings, beside the 1928 sandstone meetinghouse in Torrey. No visiting hours were found; see it from the street.',
  [W('Torrey Log Church–Schoolhouse'),L('HMdb: Torrey Log Church and Schoolhouse marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=142664')],brick='no'),
N('spring-city-historic-district','Spring City Historic District','Spring City','ut','town','standing',39.47749,-111.49432,'area','public','walk','','1859–1911',
  'Settled by Latter-day Saints in 1852, abandoned during conflict with the Utes and resettled in 1859, Spring City was largely Danish ("Little Denmark"). Nearly the whole town is a Register district, chosen as an example of the planning and building of 19th-century Latter-day Saint villages: broad streets, acre lots and houses of log, adobe and the local oolitic limestone. The 2022 survey counted about 500 buildings, outbuildings and sites, 58 percent of them contributing.',
  'Stone houses, barns and outbuildings across the grid. The two tallest buildings are the three-storey Spring City School of 1899 at 45 South 100 East, listed on its own, and the limestone meetinghouse of 1902 to 1911 on the public square on the west side of town, one of the last big buildings in Sanpete County made entirely of oolite. Nothing is left of the fort or the first log and adobe meetinghouses.',
  'A Danish pioneer village of pale limestone, nearly all of it still standing.',
  'Public streets; the houses are private, so look from the street. The pin is the old school.',
  [W('Spring City, Utah'),L('Utah State Historic Preservation Office: Spring City Historic District, additional documentation (2022)','https://history.utah.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/PRES-UT_Sanpete-County_Spring-City-HDAD_form-compressed.pdf')],brick='?'),
N('helper-museum-hotel','Western Mining and Railroad Museum (Old Helper Hotel)','Helper','ut','hotel','standing',39.68293,-110.85628,'site','open','drive','','1913–1914',
  'Helper grew up as the Denver and Rio Grande Western Railway\'s helper-engine town and a coal-mining centre, drawing Italian, Austrian, Chinese and other immigrant workers. The museum, begun in 1964, fills the Old Helper Hotel, built in 1913 to 1914, in the town\'s historic district.',
  'The 1913 hotel with a walk-through mock coal mine in the basement, the jail and smithy, themed rooms of a company store, schoolroom and veterans\' hall upstairs, a view of the rail yard from the top floors, and mining machinery outside (Helper City\'s page). The town\'s museum sells booklets for a self-guided drive up Spring Canyon\'s mining camps.',
  '',
  'Run by Helper City, on Main Street in the Helper historic district. Hours from the city\'s museum page; closed Sundays and major holidays.',
  [W('Western Mining and Railroad Museum'),L('Helper City: the Helper Museum (hours)','https://helpercity.gov/museum')],brick='?',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 11 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
# ---- east ----
N('wolfe-ranch-arches','Wolfe Ranch','Arches National Park','ut','landmark','standing',38.73642,-109.51972,'site','public','walk','','1898–1910',
  'John Wesley Wolfe, a Civil War veteran from Ohio, settled here on Salt Wash with his son Fred in 1898 and ran cattle under the Bar DX brand. When his daughter Flora arrived with her family in 1906 she made him build a better cabin with a wooden floor and real windows; six people lived in its one room, 17 by 15 feet. The family left in 1908 and sold in 1910; old maps call it Turnbow Cabin after a later owner.',
  'The 1906 one-room cabin, a 1907 root cellar beside it and a corral (NPS and Wikipedia). The dam on Salt Wash is no longer visible. The first stretch of the Delicate Arch trail passes the cabin and a petroglyph panel, and that stretch takes wheelchairs.',
  'Six people in one room, 17 feet by 15, at the foot of the Delicate Arch trail.',
  'Arches National Park (NPS); park entrance fee and any timed-entry rule not checked. Park at the Delicate Arch trailhead lot (vault toilets, no drinking water); the cabin is a short walk. Dogs are not allowed on the trail.',
  [W('Wolfe Ranch'),L('NPS: Wolfe Ranch','https://www.nps.gov/places/arch-wolfe-ranch.htm'),L('NPS: Delicate Arch trail and Wolfe Ranch trailhead','https://www.nps.gov/places/wolfe-ranch-trailhead.htm')],
  th=[38.73557,-109.5208],thn='Delicate Arch (Wolfe Ranch) trailhead parking',brick='?'),
N('dewey-bridge-ut','Dewey Bridge site','Dewey','ut','landmark','ruins',38.81194,-109.3025,'area','public','drive','burned','1916–2008',
  'A wooden-decked steel-cable suspension bridge of 1916 that carried the river road over the Colorado, for decades the longest suspension bridge in Utah and, the AP reported, its longest bridge with no supports in the water. A newer bridge took the traffic, the county took the deed in 1989 and townspeople raised money to restore it for walkers. On April 6, 2008 a brush fire started by a child playing with matches at a campsite downstream raced up the bank and burned it.',
  'The BLM says the remains of the bridge are by its Dewey Bridge recreation site, beside the present highway bridge. Photographs from 2025 on Wikimedia Commons show the steel lattice towers at the ends still standing with their cables, a path running between them, and a bronze plaque and an interpretive sign by the south end. The wooden deck is gone.',
  'Utah\'s longest suspension bridge, restored for walkers and then lost to a brush fire.',
  'Bureau of Land Management (Moab Field Office). The Dewey Bridge recreation site, on Utah 128 about 29 miles from US 191, has seven first-come campsites, toilets and a boat ramp but no water; camping fee $20 a site on the BLM\'s page ($15 at the fee station, the same page says). Do not enter the river without a life jacket: the BLM warns of dangerous undercurrents.',
  [L('BLM: Dewey Bridge Recreation Site','https://www.blm.gov/visit/dewey-bridge-recreation-site'),W('Utah State Route 128'),
   L('Moab Happenings: the loss of Dewey Bridge (2008)','https://www.moabhappenings.com/Archives/historic0805DeweyBridge.htm'),L('KSL (AP): Historic suspension bridge near Moab destroyed','https://www.ksl.com/article/3025799/historic-suspension-bridge-near-moab-destroyed')],
  th=[38.81092,-109.30804],thn='Dewey Bridge boat ramp parking',brick='no'),
N('bank-of-vernal','Bank of Vernal (the Parcel Post Bank)','Vernal','ut','civic','standing',40.45547,-109.52887,'exact','view','drive','','1916',
  'When W. H. Coltharp built this bank in 1916, freight to Vernal cost far more than the Salt Lake City bricks were worth, so he sent them by the new parcel post: about 80,000 bricks, each wrapped in newspaper, in 50-pound crates, 40 tons in all, by rail to Mack, Colorado, narrow-gauge rail to Watson, Utah, and wagon and ferry the last 65 miles (Utah Humanities). The Post Office changed its rules soon after; the Postmaster General wrote that buildings were not meant to go through the mail.',
  'The 1916 brick bank on the corner of Main Street and Vernal Avenue, still a bank (a Zions Bank branch, says HMdb), with a 1958 Daughters of Utah Pioneers marker on its north wall by the main door. Interior not checked.',
  'A bank whose bricks came through the mail, one wrapped brick at a time.',
  'A working bank at 3 West Main Street; see the outside and the marker from the sidewalk. Banking hours were not looked up.',
  [L('Utah Humanities: Vernal\'s Parcel Post Bank','https://stories.utahhumanities.org/item/1303'),L('HMdb: Parcel Post Bank marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=114421')],brick='?'),
N('josie-morris-cabin','Josie Bassett Morris Ranch','Dinosaur National Monument','ut','landmark','standing',40.4254,-109.175,'site','public','drive','','1914–1964',
  'Josie Bassett, who as a young woman on her family\'s ranch in Browns Park knew members of Butch Cassidy\'s Wild Bunch, homesteaded on Cub Creek and lived here on her own for about fifty years until her death in 1964, raising stock, crops and fruit without plumbing or electricity. The NPS says she settled in 1914; Wikipedia says she moved to the homestead in 1913 and built the present cabin with help in 1924. The land then became part of the monument.',
  'The rehabilitated cabin under the shade trees she planted, a chicken coop, remains of her irrigation ditches, old orchard trees, and in Box Canyon and Hog Canyon, reached by short trails from the lot, the wooden fences where she penned her stock (NPS).',
  '',
  'Dinosaur National Monument (NPS), at the end of Cub Creek Road, 10 miles from the Quarry Visitor Center. Parking, picnic tables and vault toilets at the lot. Monument entrance fee and road hours not checked.',
  [W('Josie Bassett'),L('NPS: Cub Creek area','https://www.nps.gov/dino/planyourvisit/cubcreek.htm'),L('NPS: Josie Morris Cabin','https://home.nps.gov/dino/learn/historyculture/josie-morris-cabin.htm'),L('NPS: Josie Morris Homestead','https://www.nps.gov/places/josiemorrishomestead.htm')],
  conf='mixed',th=[40.42575,-109.17474],thn='Josie Morris cabin and Hog Canyon trail parking',brick='no'),
# ---- north ----
N('benson-grist-mill','Benson Grist Mill','Stansbury Park','ut','industry','standing',40.650855,-112.29718,'exact','open','drive','','1854',
  'Built in 1854 by the Lee brothers for the apostle Ezra T. Benson on Twin Springs Creek, at a place that also had a sawmill and briefly served as Tooele County\'s seat. It ground wheat and corn for the valley and some California-bound emigrants until 1938, passed to Brigham Young and later J. Reuben Clark, and then stood empty and open to the weather for decades until volunteers began restoring it in 1983. One of the oldest standing buildings in western Utah, the NPS says.',
  'The rock and timber mill, with wooden pegs driven into its great beams and the milling machinery shown on tours, plus older buildings moved onto the site, a country store and a pavilion (Tooele County and Wikipedia). By the 1920s its wooden wheel and millstones had been replaced by a metal turbine and roller breakers.',
  '',
  'Owned and run by Tooele County Parks and Recreation, 325 State Route 138 at Pole Canyon Road, Stansbury Park; free parking, restrooms and drinking water (NPS). The county\'s page lists events (a summer farmers market, a fall festival, a Christmas lighting) but no regular opening hours; telephone (435) 882-7678.',
  [W('Benson Grist Mill'),L('Tooele County: Benson Grist Mill','https://bensonmill.org/?p=111'),L('NPS: Bensons Gristmill','https://www.nps.gov/places/bensons-gristmill.htm')],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('corinne-methodist-church','Corinne Methodist Episcopal Church','Corinne','ut','worship','standing',41.54748,-112.11063,'site','view','drive','','1870',
  'Corinne was a railroad town founded by non-Mormons in 1869, and this church, dedicated on September 20, 1870, was the first Methodist church in Utah. The 1969 Methodist marker calls it the oldest Protestant church building still standing in the state. Its last services were in 1957; townspeople have kept it from falling apart, and in 2020 the town planned to restore it for weddings and concerts.',
  'A one-room brick church about 27 by 50 feet on a battered stone foundation, with a door and lancet windows in Gothic arches, a small wooden bell tower over the entry and a later wooden vestibule (Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  'The oldest Protestant church still standing in Utah, in the gentile railroad town.',
  'Owned by the community of Corinne (Deseret News, 2020), at West 2300 North and North 4000 West. No visiting hours were found; see it from the street.',
  [W('Corinne Methodist Episcopal Church'),L('HMdb: Corinne Methodist Episcopal Church marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=105257'),L('Deseret News (2020): Corinne hopes to preserve its 1870 church','https://www.deseret.com/faith/2020/2/18/21138240/utah-history-corinne-methodist-episcopal-church-renovation-community-christian')],
  brick='?'),
N('box-elder-tabernacle','Box Elder Stake Tabernacle','Brigham City','ut','worship','standing',41.50542,-112.0149,'exact','view','drive','','1865–1897',
  'Brigham Young chose the hilltop and laid the cornerstones in May 1865; meetings began in the unfinished building in 1879 and it was completed with tower, gallery and buttresses and dedicated in 1890. On February 9, 1896, as people gathered, a fire in the furnace room left only blackened stone walls; within a year it was rebuilt and rededicated in 1897.',
  'The stone tabernacle with capped brick buttresses, a Gothic Revival tower and sixteen pinnacles, and woodwork from the 1897 rebuilding. It seats about 1,600, is still used by the Box Elder Stake, and Wikipedia says it opens for tours in summer. The Brigham City Temple of 2012 stands across the street.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints, on Main Street in Brigham City. No page of the church\'s own giving tour hours was read; the summer tours are from Wikipedia, and hours were not checked.',
  [W('Box Elder Stake Tabernacle')],brick='?'),
N('beehive-house-slc','Beehive House','Salt Lake City','ut','landmark','standing',40.76972,-111.88854,'exact','view','drive','','1854',
  'Built in 1854 to designs by Truman O. Angell for Brigham Young, it was his home and office as church president and the executive mansion of Utah Territory, and later a boarding house for young women. It takes its name from the beehive on its roof, an early Latter-day Saint sign of industry.',
  'The adobe and sandstone house one block east of Temple Square on South Temple, beside the Lion House. Wikipedia says it is normally a house museum with free tours by missionaries, but it has been closed for renovation since 2023, with reopening expected in 2027.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints, on South Temple Street. Closed for renovation (Wikipedia, which expects it to reopen in 2027); see the outside from the street meanwhile, and check the church\'s Temple Square pages before a trip.',
  [W('Beehive House')],brick='no'),
]
